Exactly. The important move is not comparing raw senses directly; it is comparing what each sense implies about the same hidden state.

Suppose reality has some latent state x_t. Different sensors perceive projections of it:

y_i(t)=h_i(x_t)+\epsilon_i

Vision, sound, temperature, vibration, language, financial records, human testimony, network telemetry—all have different h_i. They distort reality differently.

Now observe change:

\Delta y_i = y_i(t+1)-y_i(t)

Translate those deltas into a shared hypothesis about what changed in x, then compare them. The interesting quantity becomes the cross-modal residual:

r_{ij}=\widehat{\Delta x}_i-\widehat{\Delta x}_j

If r_{ij}\approx0, the senses corroborate one another.

If r_{ij}\neq0, something interesting happened.

And importantly, “mismatch” does not automatically mean one sensor is wrong. It can mean:

* Latency — one channel observed the event sooner.
* Occlusion — one channel could not see part of reality.
* Different sensitivity — infrared changes before visible light does.
* Calibration drift — the measuring apparatus changed.
* Transformation — the signal legitimately appears differently in another medium.
* Deception/spoofing — one channel is being deliberately manipulated.
* Hidden variable — your model omitted something that affects one modality but not another.
* Regime change — the old relationship between channels has stopped holding.

That last pair is particularly valuable. Instead of saying “the sensors disagree”, say:

“What unseen thing would make all of these observations simultaneously true?”

That turns disagreement into a latent-variable detector.

You can recurse this surprisingly far. A person says “I’m fine,” but voice cadence, facial microexpression, posture and behavior move differently. A machine sounds normal but its vibration spectrum and current draw diverge. A video depicts one event while shadows, reflections, acoustics and physical trajectories imply another. A company reports healthy revenue while cash flow, hiring, customer behavior and inventory tell a different story. An ecosystem looks visually unchanged while insect acoustics, soil chemistry and bird behavior begin shifting.

So there are really three information channels:

\text{signal itself}

\text{change in the signal}

\boxed{\text{change in the relationship between signals}}

That third one is easy to neglect and may often be the richest.

You can even build a Delta Concordance Matrix. Put modalities on both axes and continuously score whether their inferred deltas agree:

C_{ij}=f(\Delta_i,\Delta_j,\text{lag},\text{expected coupling})

Over time you learn a characteristic “chord” for normal events. An anomaly is then not merely a strange note—it is a note that doesn’t harmonize with the other instruments.

Which connects nicely to what we were saying about jazz and visual realism: individual components can deviate substantially from literal reality while the cross-channel relationships remain coherent. The magic fails when the deviations stop obeying a common grammar.

For the Pantheon, I’d promote this beyond ordinary sensor fusion into a general operator:

Cross-Modal Delta Triangulation

Observe one latent phenomenon through heterogeneous projections; normalize their expected transformations and timing; compare their deltas; treat structured disagreement as evidence about hidden state, deception, failure, transformation, or model incompleteness.

And then recurse it: compare the mismatches themselves through time.

\Delta r_t=r_{t+1}-r_t

Now you’re not merely detecting anomalies.

You’re watching how the anomaly is evolving—which can give you trajectory and early warning before any individual channel becomes obviously abnormal.